## <div style="background-color:#1a5276; color:white; padding:15px; text-align:center; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin:0; font-size:2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Make a Mola. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin:5px 0 0 0; font-size:1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Make a Mola.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Sustain conversations on a variety of topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express an opinion about a mola design (e.g., 'I think this mola is beautiful.').
            Can sustain a discussion on mola designs in collaboration with peers, using reasons and follow-up questions (e.g., 'I like this design because it is colorful. Do you like it too?').
            Can initiate a conversation about mola colors and designs and ask questions (e.g., 'What colors do you see?' 'I see red and blue.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color:#1a5276; text-align:center; margin-top:30px; margin-bottom:20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color:#1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color:#1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color:#1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various colorful mola pictures or realia (if available) around the classroom. Students walk around, observe the molas, and point to colors and designs. The teacher asks simple questions like, "What do you see?" or "What color is this mola?" to activate vocabulary and the theme 'I Can Make a Mola.'. Students respond with single words or short phrases (e.g., "Red!", "A turtle!", "Many colors!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a mola and says, "This is a mola. It has many colors: red, blue, and yellow. I like this mola because it is vibrant." The teacher then points to another mola and says, "This mola has a jaguar design. I think it is unique." The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors' by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher also models expressing preference using 'because'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'mola' and 'because' by asking:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a mola a book or a piece of art?" (Expected: A piece of art.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I like this mola because it is beautiful,' am I giving a reason or just saying 'hello'?" (Expected: Giving a reason.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a short dialogue between two friends, Ana and Luis, discussing different mola designs. The dialogue explicitly includes expressions of preference, reasons using 'because,' colors, and design vocabulary (e.g., 'turtle,' 'butterfly,' 'jaguar').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud clearly, pointing to corresponding mola images. Students listen and follow along.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Dialogue:</i>
                <br><b>Ana:</b> Look at this mola! It has many vibrant colors. What colors do you see?
                <br><b>Luis:</b> I see red and turquoise. It has a big turtle. I like this mola because it is traditional. Do you like it too?
                <br><b>Ana:</b> Yes, I do! I think it's beautiful. What about this one? It has a butterfly design.
                <br><b>Luis:</b> Oh, that's nice! I like the golden and round shapes. I think it's very unique.
                <br><b>Ana:</b> Me too! Our culture has amazing molas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short dialogue script between two friends, accompanied by visual aids (pictures of different molas with turtle, butterfly, jaguar designs).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will engage in brief descriptive exchanges about visual stimuli, expressing simple preferences and asking basic questions, enhancing their ability to discuss cultural art forms collaboratively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks gist questions after the dialogue:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are Ana and Luis talking about?" (Expected: Molas/Mola designs.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What design does Luis like first?" (Expected: Turtle design.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word do they use to say why they like something?" (Expected: Because.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing molas, expressing preferences, and giving reasons using 'because,' Present Simple ('A mola has...'), and target vocabulary (mola, creativity, project, inspiration, culture, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, paste, choose, express, illustrate, decorate, collaborate, turquoise, golden, round, vibrant, traditional, unique, carefully, skillfully, artistically, also, and, because, first, second, third, next, last). Pronunciation focus on /m/ and /k/ sounds and spelling of color words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Starters (Oral):</b> The teacher provides visual prompts (different molas) and sentence starters on the board. Students complete the sentences orally, practicing the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This mola has ______ colors." (e.g., red and blue)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like this mola because it is ______." (e.g., beautiful / vibrant / unique)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It has a ______ design." (e.g., turtle / jaguar / butterfly)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher listens for correct pronunciation of /m/ and /k/ and provides immediate feedback.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Mola Description Chain Drill (Oral):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by describing a mola image using two sentences (e.g., "This mola has red and green colors. It has a round shape."). The next student points to a different mola image and adds one new piece of information or preference, building on the previous sentence structure (e.g., "I like this mola because it is vibrant. It has a butterfly design."). This continues around the circle. The teacher guides them to use words like 'also' and 'and'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Ask & Tell" Mola Card Game:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of Mola picture cards (previously prepared or cut out). Student A picks a card, describes the mola using the learned vocabulary and grammar, and expresses a preference (e.g., "This mola has turquoise and golden colors. It has a turtle. I like it because it is traditional."). Student B listens, asks a follow-up question (e.g., "Do you like turtle designs also?" or "What colors do you see?"), and then expresses their own opinion. They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of 'because' and questions like "Do you like it too?".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Beautiful Mola".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Mola Design Showcase and Peer Discussion (Role-play/Group Discussion)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "I Can Make a Mola." and Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings or draws their "own mola design" (or uses a provided picture). They take turns acting as a "mola artist" presenting their work. The "artist" describes their mola, using colors, designs (turtle, butterfly, jaguar), and explains why they like it (e.g., "This is my mola project. It has a jaguar design. I chose red and black colors. I like it because it is powerful and unique. First, I cut the fabric, second, I sew the pieces carefully."). The other group members act as "visitors" or "fellow artists," asking questions (e.g., "What colors did you choose?", "Do you like this mola, [friend's name]?") and expressing opinions or feedback (e.g., "Your mola is very creative!", "I like your color choices because they are vibrant."). Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary and grammar, initiating and sustaining conversations about the designs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors, encourages interaction, and provides prompts if students get stuck.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist based on specific speaking criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Mola Design Showcase and Peer Discussion" (Stage 4).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student can initiate a conversation about a mola design.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student can describe a mola using at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., colors, designs like turtle/jaguar, adjectives like vibrant/unique).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student can express an opinion about a mola design using 'I like... because...'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student can ask a peer a follow-up question (e.g., "Do you like it too?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /m/ and /k/ sounds is generally intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student collaborates by acknowledging peers' contributions (e.g., "Your mola is very creative!").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written or oral self-reflection:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can talk about a mola and say what colors I see." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend why I like a mola design." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about molas or colors did you use today in your conversations?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most fun part about talking about mola designs with your friends?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <br>What worked well today? Students were highly engaged in describing and discussing molas, especially during the Mola Design Showcase. The use of visuals and sentence frames greatly supported their speaking. Many students successfully used 'because' to give reasons.
                <br>What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still need more practice initiating questions and using a wider range of adjectives. For the next lesson (Writing), I will provide sentence templates and word banks to help them transfer their oral language into written form, reinforcing the vocabulary and grammar used today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                <br>Today, students practiced speaking about mola designs. In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to write descriptions of molas and express their preferences in short paragraphs, building on their oral practice from this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>